$DIR/main.p:20:2: cannot pass fmt.Errorf("wrap: %w", makeError()) to errors.As, which keeps it after returning
    fmt.Errorf("wrap: %w", makeError()) points into the object of a temporary
    error in main's frame. The error made at main.p:20 is a temporary: nothing
    keeps it, so its object is dropped at the end of the statement, and a view
    of the object may not outlive that statement. errors.As stores it in *target
    (errors.p:127), so fmt.Errorf("wrap: %w", makeError()) would outlive main's
    frame.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Change errors.As to take a value or an owner, which it can keep: an owned[T]
    from new, a shared[T], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
